**UNVERIFIED — verdict:** I found no theorem proving the required worst-case coverage, or even a logarithmic union gain over Thue, at the relevant scales. **PROVED:** the uniform pointwise Fourier route has a sharper obstruction than \(\lambda\gtrsim\sqrt J\): even signed minorants require Fourier norm \(Hq^{-o(1)}\), leaving a factor \(J^c\) gap against square-root cancellation. **REFUTED:** “no averaged statement can yield a worst-case result” is too strong; sufficiently high centered moments would exclude every exception. **PROVED, within Theorem U’s model:** adding an ordinary Lehman prong preserves the coverage threshold, with qualifications about its number of moduli. None of these conclusions is a lower bound for general factoring algorithms.

## 1. Exact target and quantifiers

**PROVED — notation and balancing.** Put
\[
h=\lfloor H/4\rfloor,\qquad K=\left\lfloor\frac{qH}{4Y}\right\rfloor,
\]
\[
\mathcal L_Y=\{\ell\in(Y,2Y]:\ell\text{ prime},\ \ell\equiv3\pmod4\},
\]
and
\[
S_{q,h,K}=\bigcup_{1\le B\le h}
 \{x\in\mathbb F_q:|Bx\bmod q|\le K\}.
\]
Write \(C_q(a)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal L_Y}1_S(a\bar\ell)\).

On the dense-band branch, balancing the two terms of the stated cost gives
\[
Y=q^{\alpha+o(1)},\quad H=q^{\beta+o(1)},\qquad
\alpha=\frac2{7-2c},\quad
\beta=\frac{1-2c}{7-2c}.
\]
Indeed,
\[
JH^2=Y^{2-2c+o(1)},\qquad
\sqrt{\frac{HP}{Y^2}JH^2}
=P^{1/2}Y^{1/4-3c/2+o(1)}.
\]
Thus the dense-band cost exponent in \(N\) is
\[
E(c)=\frac{1-c}{7/2-c}.
\]
For \(c\le3/8\), the separate \(1/5\) fallback remains better.

**CONJECTURED — a sufficient positivity statement.** For any fixed
\[
\frac38<c<\frac12,
\]
the clean target is
\[
\boxed{\operatorname{Pos}_{\rm prime}(c):
\quad C_q(a)\ge1
\quad\text{for every sufficiently large prime }q
\text{ and every prime }q/2<a<q,}
\]
at the above scales, uniformly under the constant-factor changes in \(Y\) needed to select parameters from \(N\), rather than the hidden \(q\).

The stronger version \(\operatorname{Pos}_{\rm all}(c)\) replaces those prime \(a\) by every \(a\in\mathbb F_q^\times\).

**PROVED — implication to coverage.** A hit gives
\[
Bp\equiv \ell k\pmod q,\qquad B\le H/4,\quad |k|\le qH/(4Y).
\]
Therefore
\[
A=\frac{Bp-\ell k}{q}\in\mathbb Z,\qquad
|A|\le H/4+H/2=3H/4.
\]
Here \(A\ne0\): otherwise \(\ell\mid Bp\), impossible for \(\ell\ne p\) and \(B<\ell\). Dividing \((A,B)\) by its gcd preserves divisibility by \(\ell\). Theorem R consequently applies.

**PROVED — permissible weakenings.**

| Quantifier | What Theorem R actually needs |
|---|---|
| Every nonzero residue \(a\) | Stronger than necessary; prime \(a=p\in(q/2,q)\) suffices. |
| Every integer modulus \(q\) | Unnecessary; only prime larger factors are needed. |
| Every scale \(Y\) | Unnecessary; one public parameter schedule, or a budgeted collection of bands, suffices. |
| Every prime \(q\) | For inputs of size \(P^2\), only \(P<q\le\sqrt2P\) is relevant; the assertion must hold uniformly as \(P\) varies. |
| Almost every pair | Insufficient by itself: exceptional balanced semiprimes can determine worst-case time. |
| Almost every pair plus a fast exceptional-case algorithm | Sufficient if the exceptional-case guarantee and its cost are uniform. |

**REFUTED — the absolute claim about averaging.** An average that forces the exceptional set to be empty *does* give a worst-case theorem.

For example, let
\[
\Lambda_q=\frac{J\,|S\setminus\{0\}|}{q-1},
\]
the exact average of \(C_q(a)\) over \(a\ne0\). If
\[
\sum_{a\ne0}|C_q(a)-\Lambda_q|^{2r}<\Lambda_q^{2r},
\tag{1}
\]
then there is no zero count: one zero alone contributes \(\Lambda_q^{2r}\).

**PROVED — a concrete high-moment threshold.** A hypothetical estimate
\[
\sum_{a\ne0}|C_q(a)-\Lambda_q|^{2r}
 \ll_r q^{1+o(1)}\Lambda_q^r
\tag{2}
\]
implies positivity whenever
\[
\Lambda_q^r>q^{1+\varepsilon}.
\]
At \(c=3/8\),
\[
\Lambda_q=q^{2/25+o(1)},
\]
so \(r=13\), a **26th centered moment**, suffices:
\[
q\Lambda_q^{-13}=q^{-1/25+o(1)}\longrightarrow0.
\]
Moreover, the same moment order suffices throughout
\[
\frac38<c<\frac{19}{50},
\]
because
\[
13\frac{2-4c}{7-2c}>1.
\]
This would yield an exponent strictly below \(1/5\).

**UNVERIFIED — missing input.** I do not know an estimate such as (2) for these arithmetic configurations. Bourgain–Garaev reciprocal-energy estimates are not estimates for these centered coverage moments. Ordinary almost-all results, whose exceptional upper bound still exceeds one, do not resolve the worst case.

## 2. Tool exponents and the minorant problem

**PROVED — conversion from an additive saving.** Suppose
\[
T_q(t)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal L_Y}e_q(t\bar\ell),
\qquad
\max_{t\ne0}|T_q(t)|\le\kappa=Jq^{-\delta}.
\]
The minorant estimates proved below show that the best Fourier norm has exponent \(H\). Consequently, the uniform-bound argument needs
\[
\kappa H<\lambda q^{-o(1)}.
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\boxed{\delta>\alpha-\beta
=\frac{1+2c}{7-2c}.}
\tag{3}
\]
If the saving is written \(\kappa=J^{1-\rho+o(1)}\), this becomes
\[
\boxed{c<\rho-\frac12.}
\tag{4}
\]
At the balanced scale, a fixed \(q\)-saving \(\delta\) formally permits only
\[
c<\frac{7\delta-1}{2(1+\delta)}.
\]

**PROVED — Parseval restriction.**
\[
\sum_{t\ne0}|T_q(t)|^2=J(q-J),
\]
so
\[
\kappa\ge\sqrt{\frac{J(q-J)}{q-1}}
=J^{1/2+o(1)}.
\tag{5}
\]
Thus \(\rho\le1/2\). Equations (3)–(5) exclude **every fixed \(c>0\)** through this common-bound, absolute-value argument. This does not exclude frequency-dependent estimates or cancellation with the prescribed Fourier coefficients.

### (a) Bourgain–Garaev and composite moduli

**PROVED — reference correction.** Bourgain’s *More on the sum-product phenomenon in prime fields and its applications* is **International Journal of Number Theory 1 (2005), 1–32**, not GAFA. Its prime-variable consequence is **Theorem A.9**, giving a power saving for \(q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\le L\le q\), as explicitly recorded by Fouvry–Shparlinski on p. 297 of their published paper. **UNVERIFIED here:** direct inspection of Bourgain’s original Appendix A.1; the short bilinear range \(N_1N_2>q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\) is also recorded in Bourgain–Garaev’s introduction. [Bourgain’s publication](https://doi.org/10.1142/S1793042105000108), [Fouvry–Shparlinski, p. 297](https://www.impan.pl/shop/publication/transaction/download/product/82801).

**PROVED — relevant Bourgain–Garaev statements.** The reference is **Izvestiya: Mathematics 78 (2014), 656–707**. I checked the following statements and theorem numbers in **arXiv:1211.4184v1**; Theorem 8 also agrees with the indexed journal text. [Paper and statements](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1211.4184), [journal record](https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/im8070).

| Status | Result | Range/bound |
|---|---|---|
| PROVED | Theorem 1 | \(E_{2r}(I^{-1})\ll_r (|I|^{2r^2/(r+1)}+|I|^{2r}/q)|I|^{o(1)}\). |
| PROVED | Theorem 4, Remark 1 | \(E_{2r}(I^{-1})<|I|^{r+o(1)}\) for \(|I|<q^{1/(4r^2)}\). |
| PROVED | Theorem 7 | Weighted bilinear saving for \(|I_1|>q^{1/18}\), \(|I_2|>q^{5/12+\varepsilon}\). |
| PROVED | Theorem 8 | Explicit weighted bilinear bound for initial intervals; see the computation below. |
| PROVED | Theorem 11 | Equal lengths \(L\), \(n\ge7\), \(L^n>q^{1/3+\varepsilon}\). |
| PROVED | Theorem 12 | Equal lengths \(L>q^{C/n^2}\), with absolute \(C\), giving a power saving depending on \(n\). |
| PROVED | Theorem 16 | Integer-variable bound \(L(\log\log q)^3\log q/(\log L)^{3/2}\). |

**PROVED — composites really do supply cancellation at total length \(q^{0.32}\).** They must not be dismissed merely because no prime-variable estimate is known there.

Take two disjoint prime intervals of lengths \(q^{4/25+o(1)}\), restrict both variables to primes \(3\bmod4\), and form \(M=\ell_1\ell_2\). Their factors exceed \(H=q^{1/25+o(1)}\), and \(M=q^{8/25+o(1)}\).

In Theorem 8 take \(k_1=k_2=4\). Each factor inside its bound is
\[
\frac{L^3}{q^{1/2}}+\frac{q^{1/2}}{L^4}
=q^{-1/50+o(1)}+q^{-7/50+o(1)}.
\]
The two factors are each raised to \(1/32\). Hence
\[
\sum_{\ell_1,\ell_2}e_q(t\overline{\ell_1\ell_2})
\ll q^{-1/800+o(1)}L^2.
\tag{6}
\]
Prime restrictions cost only logarithms through the allowed bounded coefficients. Disjoint intervals ensure squarefreeness and unique products.

**PROVED — coverage conclusion from these bounds:** no positive \(c\) through the uniform Fourier argument. Even replacing \(1/800\) by ideal square-root cancellation cannot overcome (3)–(5). Root multiplicities and duplicate product representations must still be charged; \(J\) counts distinct moduli.

**PROVED — Theorem 16’s length threshold.** Its displayed bound tends to \(o(L)\) when
\[
\log L\gg(\log q)^{2/3}(\log\log q)^2.
\]
The paper states the convenient stronger sufficient range with \((\log\log q)^3\). At \(L=q^\varepsilon\), its saving is logarithmic, not a fixed power.

### (b) Prime inverses, average results, and bilinear fractions

**PROVED — pointwise prime-variable ranges.** Here \(L\) denotes an initial or dyadic length, not an arbitrary translated interval of length \(L\).

| Status | Precise reference | Bound and admissible range | First nontrivial power range |
|---|---|---|---|
| PROVED | Garaev, *Math. Notes* **88** (2010), 330–337, **Theorem 1.1** | \(L^{15/16}+q^{1/4}L^{2/3}\), times \(q^{o(1)}\), \(2\le L<q\), \(q\) prime | \(L>q^{3/4+\varepsilon}\) |
| PROVED | Fouvry–Shparlinski, *Acta Arith.* **150** (2011), 285–314, **Theorem 3.1** | Same bound, arbitrary modulus; \(q^{3/4}\le L\le q^{4/3}\) | \(L>q^{3/4+\varepsilon}\) |
| PROVED | Baker, *Acta Arith.* **156** (2012), 351–372, **Theorem 1** | \(L^{1-\varepsilon^4/2000}\); \(v q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\le L\le q^{3/4+\varepsilon}\), \(v\le L^{1/4}\), \(0<\varepsilon\le1/24\); \(v\) is the squarefull part of the modulus | \(q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\) for prime modulus |
| PROVED | Korolev, *Math. Notes* **106** (2019), 89–97, **Theorem 1** | \(\sum_{n\le L}\Lambda(n)e_q(t\bar n)\ll Lq^{-\varepsilon^2(1-7\varepsilon)}\); \(q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\le L\le q^{3/4}\), \(0<\varepsilon\le1/53\) | \(q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\) |

Sources: [Garaev, full Theorem 1.1](https://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=mzm&option_lang=rus&paperid=7829&what=fullt), [Fouvry–Shparlinski](https://www.impan.pl/shop/publication/transaction/download/product/82801), [Baker](https://www.impan.pl/shop/en/publication/transaction/download/product/82065), [Korolev, full Theorem 1](https://www.mathnet.ru/php/getFT.phtml?jrnid=mzm&option_lang=eng&paperid=12339&what=fullt).

**PROVED — explicit savings.** Writing \(L=q^\alpha\), the Garaev/Fouvry–Shparlinski saving is
\[
\delta=\min\left(\frac{\alpha}{16},
                 \frac{\alpha}{3}-\frac14\right)-o(1).
\]
Baker gives \(\delta=\alpha\varepsilon^4/2000-o(1)\); Korolev gives \(\delta=\varepsilon^2(1-7\varepsilon)\) for the weighted sum. None applies at \(\alpha=8/25\), and none supplies positivity with \(c>0\) via (3).

**PROVED — the \(3\bmod4\) restriction needs attention.** A bound over all primes cannot automatically be restricted to this subset. Baker’s general-modulus theorem handles the twist: for odd \(\ell\),
\[
\chi_4(\ell)e_q(t\bar\ell)
=-i\,e_{4q}\bigl((4t+q)\bar\ell\bigr).
\]
The new modulus has bounded squarefull part. **UNVERIFIED here:** an identical transfer of Korolev’s precise prime-modulus saving; its stated length already excludes the target.

**PROVED — averaged results have different thresholds.** Their comparison is against the trivial total \(QL\), for \(q\sim Q\).

| Status | Reference | Average bound, suppressing \(Q^\varepsilon\) | Nontrivial range |
|---|---|---|---|
| PROVED | Fouvry–Shparlinski, **Theorem 3.3** | \(Q^{13/10}L^{3/5}+Q^{13/12}L^{5/6}\) | \(L>Q^{3/4+\varepsilon}\); stated for \(1\le L\le Q^{3/2}\) |
| PROVED | Baker, **Theorem 2** | \(Q^{11/10}L^{4/5}+QL^{11/12}\) | \(L>Q^{1/2+\varepsilon}\); stated for \(Q^{1/2}\le L\le2Q\) |
| PROVED | Irving, **Theorem 1.1** | \(Q^{5/4}L^{5/8}+QL^{9/10}+Q^{7/6}L^{13/18}\), maximum over \(a\) | \(L>Q^{2/3+\varepsilon}\); stated up to \(Q^{3/2}\) |
| PROVED | Irving, **Theorem 1.3** | \((1+a/(LQ))^{1/2}(Q^{1/2}L^{11/8}+Q^{7/6}L^{2/3})(aQ)^\varepsilon\), fixed \(a>0\) | \(Q^{1/2+\varepsilon}<L<Q^{4/3-\varepsilon}\), with the displayed \(a\)-factor |

Irving’s paper is *Functiones et Approximatio Commentarii Mathematici* **51** (2014), 221–235; theorem numbers above were checked in **arXiv:1301.6372v2**. These estimates do not apply at \(L=Q^{0.32}\), even before the worst-case quantifier issue. [Irving, Theorems 1.1 and 1.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1301.6372).

**PROVED — relevant modern bilinear estimate.** Kerr–Shparlinski–Wu–Xi, *J. London Math. Soc.* **108** (2023), 578–621, **Theorem 2.4**, gives, in its \(r=q,c=1\) case,
\[
W=\sum_{m\in I}\sum_{n\le D}\alpha_m\gamma_n e_q(am\bar n)
\ll \|\alpha\|_2\|\gamma\|_\infty M^{1/2}Dq^{o(1)}
\left(M^{-1/4}+M^{-1/2}+D^{-1/2}\right).
\tag{7}
\]
It is nontrivial for bounded coefficients throughout \(M,D\ge q^\varepsilon\). There is therefore **no single \(q^{1/2}\) threshold for bilinear inverse fractions**. [Theorem 2.4, checked version v5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.05038).

**PROVED — applying (7) still misses positivity.** Use an interval minorant with Fourier length
\[
F\asymp q/K\asymp Y/H
\]
and coefficients \(O(K/q)\). Combining its frequency \(t\) with \(B\le h\) gives a numerator variable \(m=tB\ll Y\), with
\[
\|\alpha\|_2\ll q^{o(1)}(K/q)\sqrt{Fh}
\asymp q^{o(1)}H/\sqrt Y.
\]
Equation (7) then gives
\[
|E|\ll H Y^{3/4}q^{o(1)}.
\]
At the threshold this is \(q^{7/25+o(1)}\), whereas the main term is \(q^{2/25+o(1)}\). Its sufficient condition \(H>Y^{3/4+o(1)}\) lies outside the desired range.

**PROVED — the roots/inverses distinction.** Kerr–Shkredov–Shparlinski–Zaharescu, *J. Inst. Math. Jussieu* **24** (2025), 1765–1806, studies **modular roots**. For example, **Theorem 1.1** bounds the additive energy of modular square roots by
\[
\left(N^{3/2}q^{-1/2}+1\right)N^{2+o(1)}.
\]
It is not a short prime-inverse sum theorem; a “smallest prime-inverse length” cannot be assigned to it. [Published paper, Theorems 1.1–1.4](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/7781F2066CF922D2B609F39EDF98AF0F/S1474748023000397a.pdf/energy_bounds_for_modular_roots_and_their_applications.pdf).

### (c) Heath-Brown identity plus Weil/Deligne

**PROVED — failure of the usual Type I/II estimates at \(q^{0.32}\).** Completion bounds an unweighted incomplete inverse sum by \(q^{1/2+o(1)}\), already larger than the entire length.

For a Type II block,
\[
V=\sum_{m\sim M}\alpha_m\sum_{n\sim L}\beta_n e_q(a\overline{mn}),
\]
Cauchy–Schwarz and completion give
\[
\frac{|V|}{ML}
\ll q^{o(1)}
\left(L^{-1/2}+q^{1/4}M^{-1/2}\right).
\tag{8}
\]
If \(ML\le q^{8/25}\), then \(M\le q^{8/25}\), and the second term can be as large as
\[
q^{1/4-4/25}=q^{9/100}.
\]
Decomposing into more factors does not, by itself, create a long complete sum.

**PROVED — scope of that conclusion.** These elementary completion estimates give no positivity. This is not an impossibility theorem for every future argument using Deligne. Heath-Brown’s identity itself is **Lemma 1**, §2, of *Canad. J. Math.* **34** (1982), 1365–1377; it supplies the decomposition, not the missing cancellation. [Original paper](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/D6C21FF61C1489E5856AA5ED276CB0A9/S0008414X00033307a.pdf/prime-numbers-in-short-intervals-and-a-generalized-vaughan-identity.pdf).

### (d) Multiplicative characters and GRH

**PROVED, conditional on the stated character bound.** Count representations
\[
aB\equiv\ell k\pmod q,\qquad
B\le h,\quad 0<|k|\le K.
\]
Multiplicative orthogonality gives
\[
R(a)=\frac{J(2K)h}{q-1}
+\frac1{q-1}\sum_{\chi\ne\chi_0}
 \overline{\chi}(a)L(\chi)K(\chi)\overline{B(\chi)}.
\]
If \(|L(\chi)|\le\kappa_\chi\), Cauchy–Schwarz and orthogonality give
\[
|E_\chi|\ll\kappa_\chi\sqrt{Kh}.
\tag{9}
\]
For \(\kappa_\chi=Jq^{-\delta}\), positivity requires
\[
\boxed{\delta>\frac12+\alpha c.}
\tag{10}
\]

**PROVED, assuming GRH.** Square-root prime character sums give
\[
\kappa_\chi\ll Y^{1/2}q^{o(1)}.
\]
At \(c=3/8\), (10) asks for \(\delta>31/50\), while GRH gives only \(4/25\). Equivalently,
\[
E_\chi\ll H\sqrt q\,q^{o(1)}
=q^{27/50+o(1)}
\]
against a main term \(q^{2/25+o(1)}\). The generic sufficient condition is \(JKh>q^{2+o(1)}\); here \(JKh=q^{27/25+o(1)}\).

**UNVERIFIED — stronger logical implication.** This calculation establishes failure of this application of GRH. It does not prove that GRH could have no other relevant consequences. The square-root input follows from the Dirichlet explicit formula; see §11.8, equation (11.8.3), in [Granville’s treatment](https://dms.umontreal.ca/~andrew/Courses/Chapter11.pdf).

### (e) Hooley \(R^*\), nonnegative minorants, and signed minorants

**CONJECTURED — \(R^*\) and its prime analogue are different inputs.** Hooley’s \(R^*\) concerns integer-variable incomplete Kloosterman–Ramanujan sums:
\[
\sum_{n\in I,(n,q)=1}e_q(a\bar n)
\ll_\varepsilon |I|^{1/2}(a,q)^{1/2}q^\varepsilon,
\qquad 1<|I|<q.
\]
It is stated under the label **Hypothesis \(R^*\)** on p. 110 of Iwaniec, *J. Math. Soc. Japan* **34** (1982), 95–123. It does not automatically give the analogous prime sum. Even granting that stronger analogue will be insufficient below. [Iwaniec, p. 110](https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jmath1948/34/1/34_1_95/_pdf/-char/en).

**PROVED — normalization.** Define
\[
\widehat g(t)=\sum_xg(x)e_q(-tx),\qquad
A(g)=\frac1q\sum_t|\widehat g(t)|,\qquad
A_0(g)=\frac1q\sum_{t\ne0}|\widehat g(t)|.
\]
For a real minorant \(g\le1_S\),
\[
C_q(a)\ge
\frac Jq\sum_xg(x)-\kappa A_0(g).
\tag{11}
\]

**PROVED — nonnegative minorants have norm \(H\), up to logarithms, in the target range.** Assume
\[
K\ge h^3,\qquad 4Kh^3<q.
\tag{12}
\]
These conditions hold at the target scales for every fixed \(c>3/8\), and more generally on this branch for \(c>1/4\).

The second condition identifies modular additive quadruples with rational ones. For fixed denominators \(b_1,\dots,b_4\le h\), let \(L=\operatorname{lcm}(b_1,\dots,b_4)\). The numerator equation has at most
\[
O\!\left(K^3\frac{\min b_i}{L}\right)
\]
solutions: reduce modulo \(L/\min b_i\le h^3\), then use \(K\ge h^3\).

Furthermore,
\[
\sum_{b_1,\dots,b_4\le h}\frac1{\operatorname{lcm}(b_1,\dots,b_4)}
\ll(\log(2h))^{15},
\]
because its Euler factors are \(1+15/p+O(p^{-2})\). Hence
\[
E(S)\ll K^3h(\log(2h))^{15},\qquad |S|\asymp Kh.
\tag{13}
\]

If \(0\le g\le1_S\) and \(\sum g\ge|S|/2\), then
\[
\sum g^2\ge |S|/4,\qquad E(g)\le E(S).
\]
The proposed Hölder inequality therefore proves
\[
\boxed{A(g)\gg\frac{h}{(\log(2h))^{15/2}}.}
\tag{14}
\]

**PROVED — a matching upper bound up to logarithms.** When \(2Kh<q\), distinct reduced fractions \(k/b\), \(|k|\le K,b\le h\), remain distinct modulo \(q\). Möbius inversion expresses \(1_S\) as \(O(h\log(2h))\) signed dilates of interval indicators. Consequently,
\[
A(1_S)\ll h\log(2h)\log q.
\tag{15}
\]
Thus (14) answers the nonnegative-minorant question to within polylogarithmic factors in the relevant range.

**PROVED — signed minorants cannot improve the power of \(H\).** The energy argument alone does not establish this, but a different elementary argument does.

For \(t\ne0\), let \(v_b=t\bar b\bmod q\) be the centered residue. Divisor counting gives
\[
\#\{b\le U:|v_b|\le T\}
\ll (1+UT/q)q^{o(1)}.
\tag{16}
\]
Indeed, \(bv_b=t_0+nq\), with \(|n|\ll1+UT/q\), and each nonzero integer on the right has \(q^{o(1)}\) divisors.

Using
\[
\left|\sum_{|r|\le L}e_q(tr\bar b)\right|
\ll\min(L,q/|v_b|)
\]
and dyadic summation in (16), followed by the same Möbius inversion, proves
\[
\max_{t\ne0}|\widehat{1_S}(t)|
\ll (K+h)q^{o(1)}=Kq^{o(1)}.
\tag{17}
\]

Now let \(g\le1_S\) be signed, and \(m=\sum g\ge|S|/2\). Outside \(S\), \(g\le0\), so
\[
\sum g1_S\ge m.
\]
Removing the zero Fourier coefficient gives
\[
m(1-|S|/q)
\le A_0(g)\max_{t\ne0}|\widehat{1_S}(t)|.
\]
For \(Kh=o(q)\), equation (17) yields
\[
\boxed{A_0(g)\ge hq^{-o(1)}.}
\tag{18}
\]
Together with (15), this determines the optimal signed-minorant **exponent**:
\[
\inf_{\substack{g\le1_S\\ \sum g\ge|S|/2}}A(g)
=Hq^{o(1)}.
\]

**UNVERIFIED — remaining norm precision.** I have not proved that the \(q^{o(1)}\) loss in the signed lower bound can be replaced by a fixed polylogarithm. The divisor estimate used in (16) does not justify that replacement.

**PROVED — consequence for square-root cancellation.** Equations (5), (11), and (18) impose the exponent condition
\[
\lambda\gg H\sqrt J\,q^{-o(1)}.
\]
But
\[
\lambda=J^{1-2c+o(1)},\qquad
H\sqrt J=J^{1-c+o(1)}.
\]
The error budget exceeds the main term by \(J^{c-o(1)}\). This applies to signed as well as nonnegative minorants.

**REFUTED — the unqualified extension to \(c=0\).** The rational-energy transfer requires \(H^4\ll Y\); it cannot simply be used at \(H\asymp\sqrt Y\). More decisively, if \(hK\ge q\), Dirichlet gives \(S=\mathbb F_q\), and \(g=1\) has \(A(g)=1\), \(A_0(g)=0\). Suitable constant multiples of \(\sqrt Y\) reach this situation. The rigorous obstruction above concerns fixed positive power gains \(c>0\), not an impossibility of recovering Thue.

## 3. Attempts beyond linear statistics

**UNVERIFIED — outcome of the blind attempts.** I did not prove coverage with any fixed \(c>0\), or with \(H=\sqrt Y/f(Y)\), \(f(Y)\to\infty\), at the relevant polynomial relation between \(q\) and \(Y\). The following identifies the actual failures; it does not prove that these approaches can never work.

| Attempt | Status and failure |
|---|---|
| Sum-product growth | **PROVED:** growth or density below one does not imply that the specific product set contains every residue. Turning a dense set into a full field by addition changes the permitted representation. |
| Reciprocal sumsets | **PROVED:** Bourgain–Garaev Corollary 1 gives a cardinality bound, not full coverage. Even a full reciprocal-sum representation does not preserve the needed coefficient height. |
| Joint geometry of numbers | **PROVED:** intersecting the lattices replaces a determinant of size \(\ell\) by a product of moduli. Minkowski then gives a larger height. A union improvement requires information about the dependence of their first minima. |
| Pigeonhole/Ramsey on shortest vectors | **PROVED:** vectors of height \(O(\sqrt Y)\) have \(O(Y)\) possible directions, while \(J\asymp Y/\log Y\). Repetition is not forced. Coarsening directions supplies approximate parallelism, not a short vector in a required lattice. |
| Sieve over candidate \(p\) | **PROVED for the elementary implementation:** congruence correlations are controllable by direct interval counting only while the product of selected band primes is below the available level. With \(q=Y^{25/8}\), only about three factors fit below level \(q\). This does not control the enormous zero-hit tail. |
| Polynomial method | **PROVED:** at each \(\ell\), the product \(\prod_{(A,B)}(BX-AY)\) has many nonzero projective directions when \(H^2\ll\ell\). Its degree alone does not force a zero at the specified pair. CRT combines the restrictions at a modulus exponentially larger than \(q\). |

**PROVED — the reciprocal-sum height loss explicitly.** Suppose a covering theorem supplied
\[
p^{-1}=\frac1{\ell_1}+\cdots+\frac1{\ell_r}\pmod q.
\]
Clearing denominators gives
\[
pB\equiv M\pmod q,\qquad
M=\prod_i\ell_i,\quad
B=\sum_i\prod_{j\ne i}\ell_j.
\]
For comparable factors and \(M\asymp Y\), this only bounds
\[
B\ll_r Y^{1-1/r}.
\]
Already \(r=2\) gives the Thue exponent \(1/2\); it does not give \(B\le Y^{1/2-c}\). Additional cancellation or factorization of this numerator would itself require a new theorem.

**PROVED — why repeated shortest vectors also do not finish the argument.** If a particular nonzero \(v=(A,B)\) works at several primes, those primes divide \(Bp-Aq\). Its size bounds their number, but this supplies an upper bound on repetitions. It does not force any vector into the smaller box.

**CONJECTURED — a viable nonlinear target.** The centered-moment estimate (2) is one genuine route beyond bounded linear statistics. Its required moment order is
\[
r>\frac{7-2c}{2-4c}.
\]
Unlike a pointwise exponential-sum estimate at the random level, such a moment bound would control the zero-hit tail sufficiently strongly. **UNVERIFIED:** no cited reciprocal-energy or sieve theorem supplies it.

## 4. DCL plus Lehman, and other second prongs

**PROVED — union of designs, with the model hypotheses retained.** If both prongs are fixed enumerated designs satisfying the charging convention and WIN, their union is another such design. Writing \(J_{\rm tot}\) for its distinct moduli,
\[
X\ge P^{2/5-o(1)}
 \lambda_{\rm tot}^{4/5}J_{\rm tot}^{-1/5}.
\]
Thus a cost \(X=N^{1/5-\eta+o(1)}\) requires
\[
\boxed{\lambda_{\rm tot}
\le J_{\rm tot}^{1/4}N^{-5\eta/4+o(1)}.}
\tag{19}
\]
For an ordinary modulus-one Lehman prong, \(J_{\rm tot}=J_{\rm DCL}+1\).

**PROVED — the proposed Lehman fraction bound for one modulus.** Apply Theorem U with \(J_L=1\). If \(\sigma_L\) is its covered fraction, then \(\sigma_L\le\lambda_L\), and
\[
\boxed{\sigma_L\le
P^{o(1)}\frac{X^{5/4}}{P^{1/2}}
=P^{o(1)}
 \left(\frac{X}{P^{2/5}}\right)^{5/4}.}
\tag{20}
\]
At \(X=N^{1/5-\eta}\), this is \(N^{-5\eta/4+o(1)}\).

**PROVED — direct check using the corrected Lehman score.** The condition
\[
L^*(p,q)=\min_v\max\left(h(v)^2,
 |\delta_v|\sqrt{h(v)/P}\right)\le X
\]
allows
\[
h\le\sqrt X,\qquad
|\delta_v|\le X\sqrt{P/h}.
\]
There are \(O(h)\) weights at height \(h\), each covering a ratio interval of width
\[
O\!\left(\frac{X}{\sqrt P\,h^{3/2}}\right).
\]
Summing gives
\[
\frac X{\sqrt P}\sum_{h\le\sqrt X}h^{-1/2}
\ll\frac{X^{5/4}}{\sqrt P},
\]
with logarithmic losses when passing to the prime-pair population.

**REFUTED — unrestricted use of (20).** A stationary-window prong using \(J_L\) moduli has instead
\[
\sigma_L\le
P^{o(1)}X^{5/4}J_L^{1/4}/P^{1/2}.
\tag{21}
\]
Nor does Theorem U automatically encompass adaptive weights, arbitrary window rules without WIN, class groups, or every implementation called “Harvey-style.” The required statement is about the explicitly specified model.

**PROVED — residual obligation.** With the ordinary Lehman prong, the DCL prong must cover every Lehman-hard pair, with \(\lambda_{\rm DCL}\) measured over the original population and satisfying the threshold inherited from (19). The small easy-set fraction does not prove that any hard pair is covered.

**PROVED — linear families are easy.** For
\[
p=ax+b,\qquad q=cx+d,\qquad g=\gcd(a,c),
\]
choose \((A,B)=(a/g,c/g)\). Then
\[
Bp-Aq=(cb-ad)/g
\]
is constant. For fixed coefficients, the corresponding stationary square test is inexpensive. Therefore the \((x,x+1)\) barrier does not obstruct a “covered or Lehman-easy” theorem.

**PROVED — the badly approximable survivor.** Let \(\alpha\in(1,2)\) be badly approximable and take
\[
(p,q)=(x,\lfloor\alpha x\rfloor).
\]
For weights of height \(h\),
\[
|Bp-Aq|
\ge x|B-A\alpha|-|A|
\ge c_\alpha x/h-h.
\]
Throughout \(h\ll P^{1/5}\), this gives
\[
L^*(p,q)\gg
\max\left(h^2,\sqrt{P/h}\right).
\]
Minimizing the right side gives \(P^{2/5}\). Larger \(h\) already costs \(h^2\ge P^{2/5}\).

Thus a coverage-or-Lehman theorem valid for all coprime pairs must cover a sparse set
\[
\{Bx-A\lfloor\alpha x\rfloor: |A|,B\le H\}
\]
for the coprime members of this family. Such members occur infinitely often using lower continued-fraction convergents. This does not supply a prime-pair obstruction.

**UNVERIFIED — other second prongs.** I found no theorem putting every uncovered pair at \(\lambda=J^{1/4-\delta}\) inside a deterministic \(p-1\), class-group, order-based, or Hittmeir easy set below \(N^{1/5}\). Those methods have structural success conditions, but no established implication from this uncoveredness condition was located. This is a literature-status finding, not a proof of nonexistence.

**PROVED — general benchmark.** Harvey–Hittmeir, *Math. Comp.* **91** (2022), 1367–1379, **Theorem 1.1**, gives
\[
O\!\left(
N^{1/5}\log^{16/5}N/(\log\log N)^{3/5}
\right).
\]
It supplies no smaller fixed exponent for the remaining pairs. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.11105), [published reference](https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/3708).

## 5. Sharpest gap and computations

**PROVED — the single sharpest exponent comparison for the pointwise route.**
\[
\boxed{
\kappa_{\rm needed}
\ll \frac{\lambda}{H}
=J^{1/2-c+o(1)},
\qquad
\kappa_{\rm uniform}\ge J^{1/2+o(1)}.
}
\tag{22}
\]
The gap is \(J^{c-o(1)}\). At \(c=3/8\),
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
Y,J&q^{8/25+o(1)}\\
H&q^{1/25+o(1)}\\
K&q^{18/25+o(1)}\\
\lambda&q^{2/25+o(1)}\\
\kappa_{\rm needed}&q^{1/25-o(1)}\\
\text{square-root scale}&q^{4/25+o(1)}
\end{array}
\]
Equivalently, the required relative saving is
\[
\delta>\frac7{25},
\]
whereas square-root cancellation gives only \(4/25\). The discrepancy bound obtained after paying the minorant norm is \(q^{1/5+o(1)}\), versus the required \(o(q^{2/25})\): a factor \(q^{3/25}\).

**CONJECTURED — an estimate that would bypass this gap.** Cancellation in the *prescribed weighted count error*, for example
\[
C_q(a)=\Lambda_q+O\!\left(\sqrt{\Lambda_q}\,q^{o(1)}\right)
\]
uniformly in the required \(a,q\), would suffice. This is a substantially different input from a common bound for individual Fourier coefficients. **UNVERIFIED:** no standard hypothesis or theorem checked here supplies this estimate.

**PROVED — computations actually run.** I recorded the blind calculations before reading the optional notes or searching the literature. The scripts use exact rational arithmetic for exponents and rational energies, and NumPy FFT for finite-field Fourier norms:

